    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Senior Secondary Practical Chemistry|Systematic salt analysis",
        "new case",
        "Systematic salt analysis identifies ions through a planned sequence of selective reactions and confirmatory tests.",
        "Separation and control of pH or reagent conditions reduce interference. A single precipitate colour is usually insufficient to identify every possible ion in a mixture.",
        "Adding silver nitrate under appropriate acidified conditions can precipitate chloride as AgCl. Confirmatory behaviour and possible interfering ions must be considered before identifying chloride from cloudiness alone.",
        "Ag+ + Cl- -> AgCl(s)",
        "A precipitate's colour alone is not a complete identification when other ions can produce similar observations.",
        "Qualitative analysis develops selective separation and chemical identification skills.",
        listOf("Adding silver nitrate under appropriate acidified conditions can precipitate chloride as AgCl.","Confirmatory behaviour and possible interfering ions must be considered before identifying chloride from cloudiness alone.","Ag+ + Cl- -> AgCl(s)","Separation and control of pH or reagent conditions reduce interference.","A single precipitate colour is usually insufficient to identify every possible ion in a mixture.","A precipitate's colour alone is not a complete identification when other ions can produce similar observations."),
        listOf("Ag+ + Cl- -> AgCl(s)","Systematic salt analysis identifies ions through a planned sequence of selective reactions and confirmatory tests.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Senior Secondary Practical Chemistry|Organic functional-group tests",
        "new case",
        "Functional-group tests detect characteristic reactivity, but their specificity depends on the substrate and test conditions.",
        "A positive result should be interpreted alongside structure and possible interferences. Distinguishing tests often require a sequence rather than one universal reagent.",
        "An aldehyde can give a silver-mirror response with properly prepared Tollens reagent, while an ordinary ketone usually does not under the same test. Reducing sugars and other reducing substances show why the test is not unique proof of a simple aldehyde structure.",
        "functional-group inference requires a selective reaction and supporting evidence",
        "Do not identify an entire molecule from one positive colour or precipitate test.",
        "Functional-group tests support preliminary identification before spectroscopic confirmation.",
        listOf("An aldehyde can give a silver-mirror response with properly prepared Tollens reagent, while an ordinary ketone usually does not under the same test.","Reducing sugars and other reducing substances show why the test is not unique proof of a simple aldehyde structure.","functional-group inference requires a selective reaction and supporting evidence","A positive result should be interpreted alongside structure and possible interferences.","Distinguishing tests often require a sequence rather than one universal reagent.","Do not identify an entire molecule from one positive colour or precipitate test."),
