    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Senior Secondary Practical Chemistry|Inorganic and organic preparations",
        "new case",
        "A chemical preparation combines controlled reaction, isolation, purification and characterization of a target substance.",
        "Reaction yield and product identity answer different questions. Isolation conditions must suit the product's solubility, volatility and stability, and purity requires an appropriate test.",
        "Prepare a crystalline product, filter it, wash away soluble impurities and dry before weighing. A wet solid gives an artificially high apparent yield, so mass alone cannot establish successful pure-product preparation.",
        "isolated yield = dry product amount / theoretical product amount",
        "Do not count retained solvent or unreacted starting material as pure product.",
        "Preparation workflows connect synthetic transformations with reproducible isolation and quality assessment.",
        listOf("Prepare a crystalline product, filter it, wash away soluble impurities and dry before weighing.","A wet solid gives an artificially high apparent yield, so mass alone cannot establish successful pure-product preparation.","isolated yield = dry product amount / theoretical product amount","Reaction yield and product identity answer different questions.","Isolation conditions must suit the product's solubility, volatility and stability, and purity requires an appropriate test.","Do not count retained solvent or unreacted starting material as pure product."),
        listOf("isolated yield = dry product amount / theoretical product amount","A chemical preparation combines controlled reaction, isolation, purification and characterization of a target substance.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Senior Secondary Practical Chemistry|Chemical kinetics and electrochemistry experiments",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Integrated rate laws connect reactant concentration with time and allow reaction order and rate constants to be extracted from experimental data.\n\nA galvanic cell converts a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical work.",
        "For a zero-order reaction, [A]t = [A]0 - kt and [A] versus t is linear. For first order, ln[A]t = ln[A]0 - kt and ln[A] versus t is linear. The slope is -k, with units mol L-1 s-1 for zero order and s-1 for first order.\n\nOxidation occurs at the anode, reduction at the cathode, electrons travel through the wire and ions through the electrolyte or salt bridge maintain charge balance.",
        "a first-order reactant falls from 0.800 M to 0.200 M in 40.0 min. k = (2.303/40.0) log(0.800/0.200) = 0.0347 min-1.\n\nExample: in a Daniell cell, Zn oxidizes at the anode and Cu2+ reduces at the cathode; electrons flow from Zn to Cu.",
        "zero: [A]t=[A]0-kt; first: k=(2.303/t)log([A]0/[A]t)\n\nEcell = Ecathode - Eanode",
        "Do not identify order from the balanced equation; order is determined experimentally from the rate law or the correct linear concentration-time plot.\n\nOxidation is always at the anode and reduction at the cathode; do not rely only on electrode signs.",
        "Integrated laws predict shelf life, drug degradation, pollutant decay and the time required for industrial conversion.\n\nGalvanic-cell principles underpin batteries, sensors and corrosion measurements.",
        listOf("a first-order reactant falls from 0.800 M to 0.200 M in 40.0 min. k = (2.303/40.0) log(0.800/0.200) = 0.0347 min-1.","Example: in a Daniell cell, Zn oxidizes at the anode and Cu2+ reduces at the cathode","electrons flow from Zn to Cu.","zero: [A]t=[A]0-kt; first: k=(2.303/t)log([A]0/[A]t)\n\nEcell = Ecathode - Eanode","For a zero-order reaction, [A]t = [A]0 - kt and [A] versus t is linear.","For first order, ln[A]t = ln[A]0 - kt and ln[A] versus t is linear."),
        listOf("Reaction order","Linear plots","Rate constant","Cell notation","EMF","Salt bridge","Integrated rate equations","Galvanic cells")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Nuclear and Radiochemistry|Nuclear stability and decay",
        "new case",
        "Radioactive decay changes an unstable nuclide through a characteristic transformation pathway.",
        "Decay probability for an individual nucleus is stochastic, while a large population follows a statistical law under the stated model. Chemical temperature changes do not generally control ordinary alpha and beta decay rates like chemical reaction rates.",
        "For a sample with a 10-day half-life, 25% of the initial parent nuclei remain after 20 days. This statement concerns the parent population, not that every atom waits exactly 10 days before decaying.",
        "N(t) = N0 exp(-lambda t); t-half = ln(2)/lambda",
        "Half-life is not the predetermined lifetime of each individual nucleus.",
        "Decay laws support age estimation, tracer timing and radiochemical measurements.",
        listOf("For a sample with a 10-day half-life, 25% of the initial parent nuclei remain after 20 days.","This statement concerns the parent population, not that every atom waits exactly 10 days before decaying.","N(t) = N0 exp(-lambda t); t-half = ln(2)/lambda","Decay probability for an individual nucleus is stochastic, while a large population follows a statistical law under the stated model.","Chemical temperature changes do not generally control ordinary alpha and beta decay rates like chemical reaction rates.","Half-life is not the predetermined lifetime of each individual nucleus."),
        listOf("N(t) = N0 exp(-lambda t); t-half = ln(2)/lambda","Radioactive decay changes an unstable nuclide through a characteristic transformation pathway.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Nuclear and Radiochemistry|Decay kinetics and half-life",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Half-life is the time required for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay.",
        "It is constant for a given radionuclide and independent of initial amount. Radioactive decay follows first-order exponential behavior.",
        "after three half-lives, the remaining fraction is (1/2)^3 = 1/8 or 12.5%.",
        "N = N0(1/2)^(t/t1/2); t1/2 = ln2/lambda",
        "Half-life does not mean every nucleus decays at that exact time; individual decay is random.",
        "Half-life calculations guide dating, medicine dosing, waste storage and radiation safety.",
        listOf("after three half-lives, the remaining fraction is (1/2)^3 = 1/8 or 12.5%.","N = N0(1/2)^(t/t1/2); t1/2 = ln2/lambda","It is constant for a given radionuclide and independent of initial amount.","Radioactive decay follows first-order exponential behavior.","Half-life does not mean every nucleus decays at that exact time; individual decay is random."),
        listOf("Exponential decay","Decay constant","Activity","Half-life")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Nuclear and Radiochemistry|Nuclear reactions",
        "new case",
        "Nuclear reactions transform nuclides through interactions with particles or other nuclei and conserve appropriate total quantities.",
        "Write mass numbers and charges explicitly and account for emitted particles. Reaction energy depends on consistent reactant and product masses, not merely the number of visible products.",
        "In 14N + 4He -> 17O + 1H, mass numbers balance as 14+4 = 17+1 and atomic numbers as 7+2 = 8+1. This is a nuclear transmutation, not ordinary rearrangement of electron-shell bonds.",
        "sum of A and sum of Z balance across the nuclear reaction",
        "Do not apply ordinary molecular bond energies to calculate a nuclear reaction's mass-energy release.",
        "Nuclear reaction bookkeeping identifies products in isotope production and transmutation studies.",
        listOf("In 14N + 4He -> 17O + 1H, mass numbers balance as 14+4 = 17+1 and atomic numbers as 7+2 = 8+1.","This is a nuclear transmutation, not ordinary rearrangement of electron-shell bonds.","sum of A and sum of Z balance across the nuclear reaction","Write mass numbers and charges explicitly and account for emitted particles.","Reaction energy depends on consistent reactant and product masses, not merely the number of visible products.","Do not apply ordinary molecular bond energies to calculate a nuclear reaction's mass-energy release."),
        listOf("sum of A and sum of Z balance across the nuclear reaction","Nuclear reactions transform nuclides through interactions with particles or other nuclei and conserve appropriate total quantities.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Nuclear and Radiochemistry|Radiochemical methods",
        "new case",
        "Radiochemical methods use radioactive tracers or decay measurements to follow material and quantify radionuclides.",
        "Observed count rates depend on detector efficiency, background, geometry and timing. Activity is the decay rate of the sample and is not identical to a raw detector count rate.",
        "A sample gives 120 counts/s with 20 counts/s background. At 50% detection efficiency, the estimated activity from the net signal is (120-20)/0.50 = 200 decays/s under that calibration.",
        "activity estimate = net count rate / detection efficiency",
        "Do not equate uncorrected counts per second with becquerels when efficiency is less than one.",
        "Tracer and counting methods quantify small amounts of radionuclides and follow chemical separations.",
        listOf("A sample gives 120 counts/s with 20 counts/s background.","At 50% detection efficiency, the estimated activity from the net signal is (120-20)/0.50 = 200 decays/s under that calibration.","activity estimate = net count rate / detection efficiency","Observed count rates depend on detector efficiency, background, geometry and timing.","Activity is the decay rate of the sample and is not identical to a raw detector count rate.","Do not equate uncorrected counts per second with becquerels when efficiency is less than one."),
        listOf("activity estimate = net count rate / detection efficiency","Radiochemical methods use radioactive tracers or decay measurements to follow material and quantify radionuclides.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Nuclear and Radiochemistry|Applications and radiation safety",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Radiochemistry uses radioactive transformations, tracer-level separations and nuclear measurements.",
        "An isotope-dilution analysis adds a known tracer and uses activity ratios to recover analyte amount.",
        "An isotope-dilution analysis adds a known tracer and uses activity ratios to recover analyte amount.",
