    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Photochemistry|Light absorption and excited states",
        "new case",
        "Absorption creates an excited electronic population when photon energy matches an allowed transition.",
        "Excited-state relaxation can compete with chemical reaction. Excitation wavelength determines photon energy, while concentration and path length affect how many photons are absorbed.",
        "A 400 nm photon has greater energy than a 600 nm photon because energy is inversely proportional to wavelength. Absorbing the higher-energy photon does not guarantee a higher chemical product yield.",
        "E photon = hc/lambda",
        "Do not confuse photon energy with total beam intensity or with photochemical quantum yield.",
        "Excitation control selects accessible states in spectroscopy and photochemistry.",
        listOf("A 400 nm photon has greater energy than a 600 nm photon because energy is inversely proportional to wavelength.","Absorbing the higher-energy photon does not guarantee a higher chemical product yield.","E photon = hc/lambda","Excited-state relaxation can compete with chemical reaction.","Excitation wavelength determines photon energy, while concentration and path length affect how many photons are absorbed.","Do not confuse photon energy with total beam intensity or with photochemical quantum yield."),
        listOf("E photon = hc/lambda","Absorption creates an excited electronic population when photon energy matches an allowed transition.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Photochemistry|Jablonski diagrams",
        "new case",
        "A Jablonski diagram organizes electronic states and radiative and nonradiative transitions.",
        "Fluorescence commonly connects excited and ground singlet states, while intersystem crossing changes spin multiplicity and phosphorescence commonly involves a triplet-to-singlet transition. Diagram arrows represent different processes.",
        "After excitation to an upper singlet state, a molecule may relax vibrationally, fluoresce, or cross to a triplet state. A long-lived emission channel can involve phosphorescence rather than simply slower absorption.",
        "absorption, internal conversion, intersystem crossing and emission compete",
        "Do not label every downward arrow as fluorescence or ignore the spin multiplicities of the states.",
        "State diagrams connect emission lifetimes and yields with competing molecular relaxation pathways.",
        listOf("After excitation to an upper singlet state, a molecule may relax vibrationally, fluoresce, or cross to a triplet state.","A long-lived emission channel can involve phosphorescence rather than simply slower absorption.","absorption, internal conversion, intersystem crossing and emission compete","Fluorescence commonly connects excited and ground singlet states, while intersystem crossing changes spin multiplicity and phosphorescence commonly involves a triplet-to-singlet transition.","Diagram arrows represent different processes.","Do not label every downward arrow as fluorescence or ignore the spin multiplicities of the states."),
        listOf("absorption, internal conversion, intersystem crossing and emission compete","A Jablonski diagram organizes electronic states and radiative and nonradiative transitions.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Photochemistry|Quantum yield",
        "new case",
        "Quantum yield compares the number of specified photochemical or photophysical events with the number of photons absorbed.",
        "The event must be defined, such as product formation or fluorescence. Incident and absorbed photons differ, and chain reactions can yield more than one product event per absorbed photon.",
        "If 4.0 micromoles of photons are absorbed and 1.0 micromole of product forms, the product quantum yield is 0.25. Using the incident photon amount would be wrong if not all light was absorbed.",
        "Phi = specified events / photons absorbed",
        "Quantum yield is not the fraction of incident light absorbed, and it is not always restricted below one for every reaction type.",
        "Quantum yields compare competing pathways and light-use efficiency in photochemical systems.",
        listOf("If 4.0 micromoles of photons are absorbed and 1.0 micromole of product forms, the product quantum yield is 0.25.","Using the incident photon amount would be wrong if not all light was absorbed.","Phi = specified events / photons absorbed","The event must be defined, such as product formation or fluorescence.","Incident and absorbed photons differ, and chain reactions can yield more than one product event per absorbed photon.","Quantum yield is not the fraction of incident light absorbed, and it is not always restricted below one for every reaction type."),
        listOf("Phi = specified events / photons absorbed","Quantum yield compares the number of specified photochemical or photophysical events with the number of photons absorbed.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Undergraduate|Photochemistry|Photochemical reactions",
        "new case",
        "Photochemical reactions proceed from photoexcited species and can follow pathways unavailable or unfavourable from the ground state.",
        "Excitation changes electronic occupancy and reaction surfaces. Photon absorption must be linked to an actual chemical product channel rather than assumed from colour or fluorescence alone.",
        "An alkene can undergo photoisomerization because excitation changes the effective pi-bonding constraint during rotation. Product formation and relaxation compete, so complete conversion is not guaranteed.",
        "photoexcitation changes the accessible reaction pathway",
        "A light-induced reaction need not have the same selectivity as its thermal counterpart.",
        "Photochemical control enables isomerization, cycloaddition and other light-driven synthetic transformations.",
        listOf("An alkene can undergo photoisomerization because excitation changes the effective pi-bonding constraint during rotation.","Product formation and relaxation compete, so complete conversion is not guaranteed.","photoexcitation changes the accessible reaction pathway","Excitation changes electronic occupancy and reaction surfaces.","Photon absorption must be linked to an actual chemical product channel rather than assumed from colour or fluorescence alone.","A light-induced reaction need not have the same selectivity as its thermal counterpart."),
        listOf("photoexcitation changes the accessible reaction pathway","Photochemical reactions proceed from photoexcited species and can follow pathways unavailable or unfavourable from the ground state.")),
